# Runtime override of the robot config's `kinematics.lock_joints`.
#
# `lock_joints` pins named joints to a fixed value and drops them from the
# optimizable cspace: the locked value is what kinematics, the collision
# spheres and every solver see (curobo bakes it into
# `KinematicsConfig.lock_jointstate` at config load). On the Franka that is the
# two gripper fingers — their spread sets how wide the gripper's collision
# spheres are, so the value is part of the model's collision geometry, not a
# tuning knob.
#
# The request names the joints it changes, exactly like the response reports the
# effective state: `joint_names` + `positions` lock the named joints at the
# given values, `unlock_joint_names` release joints back into the optimizable
# cspace. Joints in NEITHER list keep the value already in force, so one call
# can move a single joint (or a single value) and leave the rest of the model
# alone, and successive calls compose. A joint has to be part of the robot
# model to be locked or unlocked, and cannot be in both lists at once.
#
# An empty request (restore=false, nothing named) therefore changes nothing and
# just reports the state in force — a read-only query, no rebuild.
#
# `restore: true` drops the whole override and reloads the robot YAML's own
# `lock_joints`; the other request fields are ignored.
#
# A joint can only be *unlocked* if the robot config's `cspace.joint_names`
# already lists it — that list is what defines the optimizable cspace, and a
# joint missing from it is rejected at re-parse. The bundled Franka config
# optimises the 7 arm joints, so its fingers can be re-pinned but not freed.
#
# Because the lock is baked into the parsed kinematics config, a change
# rebuilds the robot model AND every solver built from it (motion planner, IK,
# MPC, retargeter) — blocking, ~20 s. Refused while an execution goal is
# active (same guard/lock as set_collision_cache).

# Request
string[] joint_names         # joints to lock, at the matching `positions` value
float64[] positions          # value to pin each named joint at (parallel to `joint_names`)
string[] unlock_joint_names  # joints to release back to the optimizable cspace
bool restore                 # true -> drop the whole override, reload the robot YAML's own values
---
# Response
bool success          # true when the model was rebuilt (or already matched)
string message        # status
string[] joint_names  # every joint locked after the call, in robot model order
float64[] positions   # lock value of each name in `joint_names`
